3. navigate to the admin UI --> `Connections` --> select the 3 dot menu for your custom saas connector --> click `Configure` --> encounter blank screen

### Acceptance Criteria

- I can see the custom connector details configured via API in the connector management tab. This experience is similar to what we currently have for connectors configured via the UI.
- I can click on configure to see the variables and the respective configuration values.

### Screenshots

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/9750285/207949969-b53f84f3-b840-454b-8c21-954fa69f5ca3.png)
![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/9750285/207950020-dad17646-42e1-4da6-bdc7-56fab06b6a7b.png)


### Environment

`test_env`,  or any fides env with admin UI running


### Additional context

Encountered on a client environment with their custom connector and the behavior left the client in an understandably confused state.

- a BE update to include a new endpoint which can determine the secrets schema for a given saas config without relying on the saas config's template, but instead deriving it from an instantiated saas_config present in the database. this will allow us to generate a secrets schema even for "custom" saas connectors that do not have templates
this endpoint must take the connection config key as a param, rather than the connection_type param that's used now. this is because, for a "custom" connector, its connection_type is useless; we need the actual connection config instance in the db to look up its associated saas_config.
- FE update to be able to call the new endpoint above when the user clicks to edit a custom saas config.
TBD on how/whether the FE would determine its a custom saas config and call a different endpoint, or whether our new BE endpoint just handles all connection configs, whether they are custom saas or not, and returns the appropriate secret schema. Latter option seems preferable but not 100% sure it'll be feasible, requires a bit of investigation on the BE.

