Now that we use ES6 modules in the frontend everywhere, we are all set for unit testing in the FE. Before we can start to write some for Wake on LAN for example, we need to do the basic setup beforehand. That’s:

- [ ] Choosing a test runner and assertion lib
- [ ] Setting everything up and providing a runner script (`./dev-scripts/run-frontend-tests`)
- [ ] Documenting the process in the README or CONTRIBUTING
- [ ] Integrating everything into the CI

# Options
## Mocha (test runner) + NodeJS assertion lib
[Mocha](https://mochajs.org/) is a test runner that’s fast, reliable and has been around for ages. It can be used in conjunction with the [built-in NodeJS assertion library](https://nodejs.org/api/assert.html). The tests would look like this then:

```js
import assert from 'assert/strict'; // <-- NodeJS standard lib
import { add } from './add';

describe('add', () => {
  it('computes the sum of two numbers', () => {
    const result = add(1, 2)
    assert.strictEqual(result, 3)
  })
})
```

## Mocha (test runner) + Jasmine (assertion lib)
Alternatively, if we prefer to write our assertions in a more behaviour-driven style, we can use something like [Jasmine](https://jasmine.github.io/).

```js
import expect from 'jasmine'; // <-- Third-party lib
import { add } from './add';

describe('add', () => {
  it('computes the sum of two numbers', () => {
    const result = add(1, 2)
    expect(result).toBe(3) // <-- Behaviour driven API style
  })
})
```

## Jest
There are also all-in-one libraries like [Jest](https://jestjs.io/), that bundle a test runner, assertion lib, coverage analysis, etc. Jest has a stupendous startup time, however, which I find super annoying.

# Proposal
My personal preference is Mocha (since it’s simple and just works well) and the NodeJS assertions (since they are sufficient and come for free).

